Reports of protest placed in Belgium: 19 articles from 19 outlets on 25 Oct 2026, a surge in reporting, not in protest

19 articles from 19 outlets on 25 Oct 2026 (UTC), against a 30-day median of 2.5 a day (robust spread 2.22); 11 of them coded as violent protest or riot (CAMEO 145).

What this measures: news articles in which GDELT's machines coded a protest (CAMEO root 14) that GDELT places in Belgium, each article counted once a day. It is a surge in reporting, not in protest: it does not say that more people protested, or that protests grew.

What else explains it, as well as more protest:
- one big event, covered by many outlets at once (a news cycle);
- a story about the past told now: an anniversary, a trial, an arrest for an old crime;
- a story that names Belgium or its people but happened elsewhere: GDELT places a story by the places it names, and can place it wrongly;
- a change in what GDELT reads, or how it codes it;
- protests elsewhere about this country (outside its embassies, by its diaspora), placed here by the places they name;
- a government's diplomatic protest ("Belgium lodges a protest with ..."), which GDELT codes as a protest.
Read the articles before reading anything into the count.

Outlets with the most articles: abc17news.com (1), akipress.com (1), azernews.az (1), bankingnews.gr (1), businesstimes.com.sg (1), cyprus-mail.com (1). No outlet had more than 25% of them.
Articles (GDELT gives links, not headlines): https://news.az/news/azerbaijani-families-of-missing-persons-hold-series-of-peaceful-protests-in-brussels; https://www.universityworldnews.com/post.php?story=20261024160108215; https://www.gdnonline.com:443/Details/1410752; http://www.taiwansun.com/news/279396650/hundreds-of-thousands-march-across-france; https://akipress.com/news:927115:France_sees_another_weekend_of_mass_protests_with_nearly_1,000_detained/
